  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday morning through next Sunday)\nIssued at 310 PM EDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nBy Wed morning a cold front will be As the long term period begins, \na colds front will move south into North GA and push rapidly \nthrough the area. Expecting a line of showers and thunderstorms to \ndevelop with this front and move south through the day Wednesday. \nA few of these storms may be strong and capable of producing gusty \nwinds, frequent lightning strikes, and periods of heavy rain. We are \ncurrently under a Marginal risk across portions of central GA so will \nDefinitely keep an eye on how things develop as we get closer to \nWed. With the front moving through the area during the day we will \nalso have the diurnally driven instability to deal with and things \ntrailing off after sunset. The models show this front moving south \nof the area by 00z Thu with a much drier airmass moving in behind \nit. Drier air associated with a high pressure ridge builds southeast \nout of the western great lake states clearing things out for North \nand Central GA Thu/Fri. Temps are not expected to cool to much with \nhigh temps still expected in the upper 80s to middle 90s but the \nApparent T (feels like temps) will be  much lower.  \n\nBy Sat the next frontal boundary moving east out of the lower to mid \nMS river valley moves into W GA just before daybreak. The main low \npressure center moves east into western KY at the same time. With \nthe close proximity of the low center to NW GA this frontal boundary \nshould stay fairly organized as it moves through the region. The \nfront exits the state Sun morning as the main low center moves NE \ninto the Central/Eastern Great Lake states. Right now this is Days 6 \nand 7 so things may change as we get closer.",
